<div class="container"><div class="lesson-body">
<h2>Copula Theory — Sklar's Theorem, Archimedean Copulas & Tail Dependence</h2>
<p>Copula theory studies functions that join multivariate distribution functions to their one-dimensional margins. A <strong>copula</strong> C: [0,1]^d → [0,1] is a multivariate distribution with uniform margins. <strong>Sklar's theorem</strong> (1959) is the fundamental result: any multivariate distribution F with margins F₁,...,F_d can be written as F(x₁,...,x_d) = C(F₁(x₁),...,F_d(x_d)) for some copula C, uniquely when all margins are continuous. Copulas separate the dependence structure from the marginal distributions, making them indispensable in modern statistics, finance, and risk management. The <strong>Archimedean copulas</strong> (Clayton, Gumbel, Frank) are constructed via a generator φ(t) and capture asymmetric tail dependence. The <strong>elliptical copulas</strong> (Gaussian, Student-t) generalize the correlation structure of multivariate normal and t-distributions. <strong>Tail dependence coefficients</strong> measure the probability of joint extremes, and <strong>vine copulas</strong> extend the construction to high dimensions via pair-copula decompositions.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Copulas separate the dependence structure from the marginal distributions."</div><div class="attribution">— Abe Sklar</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 Sklar's Theorem and Basic Properties</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Foundations</div><div class="story-title">Definition of a d-copula, Sklar's Theorem, and the Fréchet-Hoeffding Bounds</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>d-copula</strong> C: [0,1]^d → [0,1] satisfies three conditions: (1) C(1,...,1,u_i,1,...,1) = u_i for each i — the margins are uniform; (2) C is <strong>d-increasing</strong> — for every hyperrectangle, the C-volume is non-negative; (3) C(u) = 0 if any coordinate is 0. <strong>Sklar's theorem</strong> (1959): for any joint distribution F with marginal distributions F₁,...,F_d, there exists a copula C such that F(x₁,...,x_d) = C(F₁(x₁),...,F_d(x_d)). If all margins are continuous, C is uniquely determined on Ran F₁ × ... × Ran F_d. Conversely, any copula C and any univariate margins F_i combine to give a valid joint distribution F. The <strong>Frobenius-Fréchet-Hoeffding bounds</strong>: for any copula C, W(u) ≤ C(u) ≤ M(u) where W(u₁,...,u_d) = max(Σ u_i − d + 1, 0) and M(u₁,...,u_d) = min(u₁,...,u_d). In the bivariate case, W(u,v) = max(u+v−1, 0) and M(u,v) = min(u,v). The <strong>survival copula</strong> is Ĉ(u) = 1 − C(1−u₁,...,1−u_d). The joint survival function is F̄(x) = Ĉ(F̄₁(x₁),...,F̄_d(x_d)). The <strong>copula density</strong> for a bivariate copula is c(u,v) = ∂²C(u,v)/∂u∂v when the second mixed partial derivative exists and is continuous.</div><div class="story-moral">Sklar's theorem shows that any multivariate distribution can be decomposed into its marginal distributions and a copula that captures all dependence — this separation is the core insight of copula theory.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 Archimedean Copulas</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Archimedean</div><div class="story-title">Generator Functions, Clayton, Gumbel, Frank Copulas, and Kendall's τ</div><div class="story-text">An <strong>Archimedean copula</strong> has the form C(u,v) = φ^{−1}(φ(u) + φ(v)), where φ: [0,1] → [0,∞] is a <strong>generator</strong> — a continuous, strictly decreasing, convex function with φ(1) = 0. The generator φ must be <strong>d-monotone</strong> for d-dimensional extensions. The three classical families: (1) <strong>Clayton copula</strong>: φ(t) = (t^{−θ} − 1)/θ for θ > 0, yielding C_θ(u,v) = max(u^{−θ} + v^{−θ} − 1, 0)^{−1/θ}. Clayton has <strong>lower tail dependence</strong> λ_L = 2^{−1/θ} and zero upper tail dependence. (2) <strong>Gumbel copula</strong>: φ(t) = (−log t)^θ for θ ≥ 1, giving C_θ(u,v) = exp(−((−log u)^θ + (−log v)^θ)^{1/θ}). Gumbel has <strong>upper tail dependence</strong> λ_U = 2 − 2^{1/θ} and no lower tail dependence. (3) <strong>Frank copula</strong>: φ(t) = −log((e^{−θt} − 1)/(e^{−θ} − 1)) for θ ∈ ℝ\{0}, with C_θ(u,v) = −(1/θ) log(1 + (e^{−θu} − 1)(e^{−θv} − 1)/(e^{−θ} − 1)). Frank has <strong>no tail dependence</strong> (λ_U = λ_L = 0). <strong>Kendall's τ</strong> for Archimedean copulas: τ = 1 + 4∫₀¹ φ(t)/φ'(t) dt. In closed form: Clayton τ = θ/(θ+2); Gumbel τ = (θ−1)/θ; Frank τ = 1 − (4/θ)(1 − D₁(θ)), where D₁(θ) = (1/θ)∫₀^θ t/(e^t − 1) dt is the <strong>Debye function</strong>. The <strong>Archimax</strong> family generalizes Archimedean copulas by incorporating a dependence function A from extreme-value theory.</div><div class="story-moral">Archimedean copulas are constructed from a single generator function and capture a wide range of dependence structures, from lower tail dependence (Clayton) to upper tail dependence (Gumbel) to symmetric weak dependence (Frank).</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 Elliptical Copulas</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Elliptical</div><div class="story-title">Gaussian Copula, Student-t Copula, and Tail Dependence Properties</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Elliptical copulas</strong> are derived from elliptical distributions — the multivariate normal and Student-t being the most important. The <strong>Gaussian copula</strong> (or normal copula): C_R^{Gauss}(u,v) = Φ_R(Φ^{−1}(u), Φ^{−1}(v)), where Φ_R is the bivariate normal distribution function with correlation R and Φ^{−1} is the quantile function of the standard normal. In d dimensions: C_R^{Gauss}(u) = Φ_R(Φ^{−1}(u₁), ..., Φ^{−1}(u_d)). To simulate: draw Z = (Z₁,...,Z_d) ∼ N(0,R), then set U_i = Φ(Z_i). The Gaussian copula has <strong>no tail dependence</strong> — λ_U = λ_L = 0 for |R| < 1, meaning extreme events are asymptotically independent regardless of correlation. This is a serious limitation for financial applications where asset returns exhibit joint extremes. The <strong>Student-t copula</strong>: C_{R,ν}^{t}(u,v) = t_{R,ν}(t_ν^{−1}(u), t_ν^{−1}(v)), where t_{R,ν} is the bivariate t-distribution with ν degrees of freedom and correlation R, and t_ν^{−1} is the quantile function of the univariate t. The t-copula has <strong>symmetric tail dependence</strong>: λ_U = λ_L = 2·t_{ν+1}(−√((ν+1)(1−R)/(1+R))). As ν → ∞, the t-copula approaches the Gaussian copula and tail dependence vanishes. For small ν, the tail dependence is substantial. The t-copula better captures <strong>extremal dependence</strong> in financial return data. Elliptical copulas allow arbitrary correlation matrices, making them flexible for high-dimensional problems. The copula density for the Gaussian copula is c_R(u,v) = (1/√(1−R²))·exp(−(R²(Φ^{−1}(u)² + Φ^{−1}(v)²) − 2RΦ^{−1}(u)Φ^{−1}(v))/(2(1−R²))).</div><div class="story-moral">Elliptical copulas are the dependence structures of elliptical distributions; the Gaussian copula lacks tail dependence while the Student-t copula captures symmetric tail dependence, making it more suitable for risk management.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 Tail Dependence and Applications</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Applications</div><div class="story-title">Tail Dependence Coefficients, Empirical Copulas, Vine Copulas, and Financial Risk</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>tail dependence coefficients</strong> quantify the likelihood of joint extreme events: the upper tail dependence λ_U = lim_{u→1⁻} (1 − 2u + C(u,u))/(1 − u) = lim_{u→1⁻} P[U > u | V > u], and the lower tail dependence λ_L = lim_{u→0⁺} C(u,u)/u = lim_{u→0⁺} P[U ≤ u | V ≤ u]. For <strong>extreme-value copulas</strong>, C(u,v) = exp((log u + log v)·A(log u/(log u + log v))) where A: [0,1] → [1/2, 1] is a convex <strong>Pickands dependence function</strong> satisfying max(t, 1−t) ≤ A(t) ≤ 1. The limiting copula of componentwise block maxima under linear normalization is an extreme-value copula. The <strong>empirical copula</strong>: C_n(u,v) = (1/n) Σ_{i=1}^{n} I(U_i ≤ u, V_i ≤ v) where U_i, V_i are ranks scaled to [0,1]. Goodness-of-fit tests use the <strong>Cramér-von Mises statistic</strong> S_n = n∫_{[0,1]²} (C_n(u,v) − C_{θ_n}(u,v))² dC_n(u,v). <strong>Portfolio risk modelling</strong>: copulas model joint dependence of asset returns beyond linear correlation. A typical workflow: fit marginal distributions (e.g., GARCH with t-innovations), select and estimate a copula (e.g., t-copula), simulate joint scenarios via Monte Carlo, and compute Portfolio Value-at-Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall. The t-copula captures the <strong>tail risk</strong> that the Gaussian copula underestimates. <strong>Vine copulas</strong> (pair-copula constructions, Joe 1996, Bedford-Cooke 2002) decompose a d-dimensional density into a cascade of bivariate copulas on conditional distributions. <strong>D-vines</strong> and <strong>C-vines</strong> are two regular vine structures. <strong>Time-varying copulas</strong> (Patton 2006) allow copula parameters — e.g., the correlation in the Gaussian copula — to evolve as functions of past data: R_t = Λ(ω + α·Φ^{−1}(u_{t−1})Φ^{−1}(v_{t−1}) + β·R_{t−1}), where Λ(x) = (1−e^{−x})/(1+e^{−x}) keeps R_t in (−1, 1). Applications span <strong>hydrology</strong> (joint distribution of flood peak and volume), <strong>survival analysis</strong> (dependent censoring), and <strong>insurance</strong> (modeling claim severity dependence).</div><div class="story-moral">Tail dependence coefficients measure the strength of joint extremes — the fundamental quantity in risk management — while vine copulas and time-varying copulas provide flexible tools for high-dimensional and dynamic dependence modelling.</div></div>
