 function A(t) for an extreme-value copula satisfies:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"max(t, 1−t) ≤ A(t) ≤ 1",c:true},{l:"B",t:"0 ≤ A(t) ≤ 1",c:false},{l:"C",t:"A(t) = t",c:false},{l:"D",t:"A(t) ≥ 1",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Pickands dependence function</strong> A(t) is convex and satisfies max(t, 1−t) ≤ A(t) ≤ 1 for t ∈ [0,1], with A(0) = A(1) = 1 and independence corresponding to A(t) = 1."},{id:43743,text:"🔥 In vine copula constructions, a d-dimensional density is decomposed into:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"A cascade of bivariate copulas on conditional distributions",c:true},{l:"B",t:"A single d-dimensional copula",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The product of univariate densities",c:false},{l:"D",t:"A hierarchical Archimedean structure only",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Vine copulas</strong> (pair-copula constructions) decompose a d-dimensional density into d(d−1)/2 bivariate copulas on conditional distributions, organized in a regular vine tree structure (D-vine or C-vine)."},{id:43744,text:"🔥 A key limitation of the Gaussian copula in financial risk modelling is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"It underestimates tail risk (no tail dependence)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"It overestimates extreme co-movements",c:false},{l:"C",t:"It cannot model negative correlation",c:false},{l:"D",t:"It requires margins to be normal",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Gaussian copula</strong> has no tail dependence for |R| < 1, so it underestimates the probability of joint extreme events — a critical flaw for portfolio risk management where tail risk is paramount."}];
